---
code: role-defined-below-its-sites
when-fires: a BOOK defines a command-keyed role function (`<family>__predict` / `__disturbs` / `__is_converged`) at a point where command sites naming that same family already sit ABOVE it, and nothing else is live for that name at those sites. cli/main.rs positional_loading_notices, one mint per late definition, spanned at the definition's name; `{sites}` counts the sites that lost their answer. Never fires when another unit's definition IS live at those sites — that world is either served or contested, and this note would be noise in both.
why: `28K` §2 rul-visibility-is-full-positional (ACKED spike-tier, human-typed 2026-07-31). Every site-keyed act reads the function environment AS OF THE SITE'S POSITION, so a definition a shell executing top-to-bottom has not reached yet licenses NOTHING above itself — no elision, no guard, no vouch. That is the design's named, accepted consequence, chosen because expectation-matching beats maximal ergonomics: the naive model of Dorc as a stupid guard-inserter, whose inserted text cannot see a definition loaded below it, now holds uniformly. The cost lands on the stage-3 bottom-appender (`28M` §6, rent-climbed), and this note is their recovery — the remediation is to move the definition above its sites, which costs one cut-and-paste and is a change the engine is uniquely positioned to notice. NOTE severity, never warning: the book is correct sh, applies unchanged, and nothing is wrong; the aid plane is naming value the admin could have (`two-plane-aid-law`).
---
-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
yum install alpha
yum install beta
yum__is_converged() { yum status "$2"; }
yum install gamma

-- replay --
$ dorc plan --book=book.sh > /dev/null
reserved: warning[reserved-namespace-squat]: Book function `yum__is_converged`
sits in the `__is_converged` namespace reserved for oracle functions; Dorc
treats it as ordinary shell and runs it verbatim, but a shipped oracle preamble
defining the same name would collide with it. Rename it to stay clear of
`*__is_converged`.
      [ book.sh:4:1
   4 | yum__is_converged() { yum status "$2"; }
     | \_______________/
loading: note[role-defined-below-its-sites]: `yum__is_converged` is defined
below 3 of its family's sites, and a definition licenses nothing above itself,
so those sites run as if it were absent -- moving it above them is one
cut-and-paste.
      [ book.sh:4:1
   4 | yum__is_converged() { yum status "$2"; }
     | \_______________/
classify: note[cmdsub-operand-top]: operand 2 is an unresolvable positional
parameter (`$@`/`$*`/`$N`), so Dorc cannot know its value until the command runs
on the host -- there is nothing to resolve and no read-only probe to check. Dorc
elides a command only when it can prove the command's effect is already in
place, so this one is left to run on every apply.
      [ book.sh:4:34
   4 | yum__is_converged() { yum status "$2"; }
     |                                  \__/

   = repair:  Give the operand a value Dorc can resolve statically -- a literal,
              or a variable assigned from one -- so Dorc can probe it and elide
              the command once it has converged. If the value must stay dynamic,
              load an oracle that vouches for this command's convergence, and
              Dorc will guard the command instead of running it every time.
probe: note[site-unresolvable]: sm 2 sites run unprobed (no read-only check
could be shipped): `yum install alpha`, `yum install beta` -- run `dorc why` for
the per-site detail (the apply runs each anyway, to stay safe)
      [ book.sh:2:1
   2 | yum install alpha
     | \_______________/

   = help:  sm site runs `yum install alpha`
why: ran because operand 2 is a command-substitution `$(...)` or runtime-dynamic value -- its value couldn't be resolved (first seen at 4:23 `yum status "$2"`); so dorc runs it, to stay safe (when unsure, run). to skip it, make the operand a literal Dorc can resolve+probe [resolve-dynamism]
hint: 'yum' (line 2) is unmodeled: it is the first wall -- an oracle vouching its convergence would elide it when converged; 1 more unmodeled wall -- dorc why
dorc: run `dorc why` for the per-site cause-chains, or `dorc why book.sh:N` to query a source line
dorc: plan-summary sites=3 elide=0 omit=0 guard=0 run=3 may-alias=0
dorc: decision-digest 6913e316c8418773

$ dorc-loom --this vars
case: role-defined-below-its-sites
{{name}} = "yum__is_converged"
{{role}} = "__is_converged"
{{sites}} = "3"
{{position}} = "operand 2"
{{cause}} = "an unresolvable positional\nparameter (`$@`/`$*`/`$N`)"
{{count}} = "2"
{{site_word}} = "sites"
{{v0}} = "book.sh"
{{v1}} = "0"
{{v2}} = "0"
{{v3}} = "0"
{{v4}} = "3"
{{v5}} = "0"
